    "final_message": "The red acceptance test is written and fails at the story's *then*, as required. I ran `scripts/verify story-drafting` once. It passed tessercheck with zero findings, `mypy --strict`, and the non-root tests. It then served the host on :9310, registered the Restate endpoint, and failed only in the root tier. The work is committed as `3beea32` on the worktree branch.\n\n**Spec** (`<repo>/.baseline-runs/red-v6-1/spec.toml`):\n- The app and the bounded context are both `story_drafting`.\n- The aggregate is `Jtbd`, with identity `jtbd_id` and one field, `job`.\n- Operations: `submit_jtbd` (write), `check_status` (read), `issue_jtbd_id`, `work_through_jtbd` (the workflow), `record_jtbd` (the action), and `save_jtbd`/`load_jtbd` on the `jtbds` collection.\n- `host = \"http\"`, `storage = \"memory\"`.\n- `calling_mode = \"start\"`, because this workflow will go on to wait for a person's pick, and a submit that waited for the workflow to finish would never return.\n- I left `step` out of the aggregate's fields on purpose. Every aggregate field becomes part of the submit body, and the developer doesn't submit a step. So the generated read answers only `jtbd_id` and `job`: a submitted JTBD has no status yet.\n\n**Test:** `story-drafting/tests/test_acceptance.py`, `TestSubmittingAJtbd::test_a_jtbd_checked_on_right_after_submitting_is_drafting`. It uses only the host's HTTP routes:\n1. `POST /jtbds` with `{\"job\": ...}` answers 201 and a `jtbd_id`.\n2. `GET /jtbds/{jtbd_id}` is read back until it answers 200. With `start`, a read right after the submit can answer 500 until the action has saved, so the test retries for up to 30 seconds, 0.2 seconds apart.\n3. It asserts that the two statuses are 201 and 200, then that the answer holds this `jtbd_id` with `step == \"drafting\"`.\n\nBecause of that retry, the test waits for the read to answer rather than checking only once, which is looser than the story's \"right away\". A version that reads once would sometimes fail"
  },
